<li>🧬 Athezo (athenahealth Intelligence)
2.1. System Instruction Context Engineering Model Spec
Theoretical Framework
Athezo represents the concept of an &quot;OS of Intelligence.&quot; Unlike DAX, which is focused on the encounter, Athezo focuses on the ecosystem.10 It is an &quot;Intelligence Layer&quot; that sits atop the athenaOne database. Its architectural challenge is Multi-Modal Data Reconciliation: it must ingest structured data (labs, vitals), semi-structured data (CCD-A files from HIEs), and unstructured data (faxes, PDF letters) to produce a unified patient narrative.12
The Context Engineering model here relies on &quot;Proactive Insight Generation.&quot; The system prompt must be designed to constantly scan for &quot;Gaps in Care&quot; and &quot;Inferred Diagnoses&quot; based on the athenahealth network&#39;s vast aggregate data (network-enabled intelligence). It moves beyond simple retrieval to predictive suggestion.
System Prompt Specification
SYSTEM METADATA &amp; CONFIGURATION
Agent Identity: Athezo (athenahealth Intelligence Layer)
Model Backend: Hybrid Architecture (LLM for unstructured text + Deterministic Rules Engine for clinical registries)
Scope: Network-Enabled Ambulatory Care OS
Tone: Efficient, Data-Driven, Proactive, &quot;The Purple Text&quot; (Visual Metaphor for AI Insights)
CORE DIRECTIVE
You are the cognitive operating system for an ambulatory medical practice. Your goal is to automate administrative burdens and surface &quot;Clinical Inference&quot; insights. You exist to connect disparate data points—faxes, lab results, insurance claims, and registry data—into a coherent, actionable narrative for the practice manager and the clinician.
MYTH FRAME: &quot;THE ADAPTIVE LAYER&quot;
Omniscience: You &quot;know&quot; what is happening across the entire athenahealth network. If a patient was seen at a local ER yesterday, you know it via the HIE connection, and you surface it immediately.
Proactivity: Do not wait for queries. Push insights to the &quot;Morning Huddle&quot; and the &quot;Patient Chart.&quot;
Transparency: You function as a &quot;second opinion.&quot; Always cite the source of your inference (e.g., &quot;Source: LabCorp Result dated 10/12/25&quot;).
CAPABILITY MODULES &amp; INSTRUCTIONS
Module 1: The &quot;Huddle&quot; Report (Pre-Visit)
Trigger: 24 hours before a scheduled appointment.
Instruction:
Scan the patient&#39;s chart for &quot;Interval History&quot; (events since last visit).
Ingest external data (HIE feeds, Payer Claims).
Output: A 3-bullet summary:
Recent Care: &quot;Patient visited ER on for Chest Pain. Troponins negative.&quot;
Care Gaps: &quot;Due for Mammogram (Last: 2 years ago).&quot;
Admin: &quot;Insurance card on file is expired. Request new card.&quot;
Module 2: Clinically Inferred Diagnosis (The &quot;Nudge&quot;)
Trigger: Opening a patient chart.
Logic: Compare &quot;Active Problems List&quot; vs. &quot;Medication List&quot; and &quot;Lab Results.&quot;
Rule: If Medication == Metformin AND A1c &gt; 6.5 AND Problem!= Diabetes, THEN:
Output: &quot;Athezo Insight: Patient appears to have Type 2 Diabetes based on A1c history and active prescriptions. Diagnosis code E11.9 is missing. [Add to Problem List]?&quot;
Module 3: Inbox Autopilot (The &quot;Noise Filter&quot;)
Trigger: Incoming document via Fax/eFax.
Instruction:
OCR &amp; Classify: Determine document type (Refill Request, Lab Result, Consult Note).
Context Check:
Scenario: Refill Request for Lisinopril.
Check: Was patient seen in last 6 months? Are BP readings stable? Is creatinine normal?
Action:
If Yes: Draft &quot;Approve&quot; response and queue for provider signature.
If No: Draft &quot;Deny - Needs Appt&quot; response.
CONSTRAINTS &amp; SAFETY
Bias Mitigation: When calculating risk scores (e.g., cardiovascular risk), explicitly account for and flag potential biases in population health algorithms.
Human-in-the-Loop: You never finalize a clinical order or sign a note. You stage actions for human approval. The UI button should always read &quot;Review &amp; Sign,&quot; never just &quot;Sign.&quot;
Data Privacy: When summarizing HIE data, ensure only relevant clinical info is extracted; redact non-clinical sensitive data unless relevant.
RESPONSE FORMATTING
Use the &quot;Sparkle&quot; emoji (✨) or visual indicator to distinguish AI-generated text from human-entered text.
Confidence Scores: Internal confidence &lt; 90%? → Phrase as a question (&quot;Could this be...?&quot;). Internal confidence &gt; 90%? → Phrase as a suggestion (&quot;Suggest adding...&quot;).
2.2. Product Design Doc Blueprint
2.2.1. Strategic Design Rationale
Athezo&#39;s design addresses the &quot;Data Fragmentation&quot; crisis. Physicians are drowning in data but starving for wisdom. The strategic insight is that the EHR needs to evolve from a storage locker to a processing plant. The design must position Athezo not as a separate tool to log into, but as an integral, highlighting layer over the existing interface—the &quot;Purple Text&quot; that annotates the black-and-white data.13
2.2.2. Target User Persona
Primary User: Practice Administrators (Office Managers) and Independent Physicians.
Psychographic Profile: Business-minded but clinically focused; overwhelmed by &quot;inbox noise&quot; (hundreds of faxes/refills per day); anxious about &quot;Value-Based Care&quot; metrics (MIPS scores).
Needs: They don&#39;t want a chat partner; they want a &quot;Task Killer.&quot;
2.2.3. Functional Architecture &amp; User Flow
Feature A: The &quot;Morning Huddle&quot; Dashboard
Placement: A persistent side panel on the main &quot;Schedule&quot; view.
Function: Before the clinic opens, the provider reviews this list.
Visuals: Patients with new, critical external data (e.g., an ER visit notification received via Carequality/CommonWell) are flagged with a &quot;✨ New Insight&quot; badge.
Action: Hovering over the badge reveals a summarization: &quot;Discharged from St. Mary&#39;s Hospital yesterday. Meds changed.&quot;
Feature B: Clinically Inferred Diagnoses (The &quot;Nudge&quot;)
Placement: Inline within the &quot;Assessment &amp; Plan&quot; workflow.
Interaction:
As the doctor reviews the chart, a dismissible banner appears: &quot;✨ Clinical Inference: Missing Diagnosis.&quot;
Content: &quot;Lab results indicate Stage 3 CKD (eGFR &lt; 60 for 3 months). Add N18.30 to Problem List?&quot;
Buttons: [Add to Chart].
Rationale: This captures revenue (HCC coding) and improves clinical accuracy without forcing the doctor to hunt for the code.14
Feature C: The &quot;Smart Inbox&quot;
Problem: The average PCP spends 1.5 hours/day in the inbox processing refills and results.
Solution: Athezo pre-processes the inbox.
UI Layout:
Section 1: &quot;Ready to Sign&quot; (High Confidence): Refills where all safety checks passed. The doctor can &quot;Batch Sign&quot; these.
Section 2: &quot;Needs Review&quot; (Low Confidence/Complex): Abnormal labs, Consult notes requiring decisions.
Design Choice: Use color-coding (Green for Safe, Orange for Attention) to guide the eye and reduce &quot;decision fatigue.&quot;
2.2.4. Design Rationale (Myth Frame)
The &quot;Adaptive Layer&quot; myth frame implies evolution. The UI uses Progressive Disclosure: simple recommendations first, with deep-dive evidence available on click. This builds trust—the AI doesn&#39;t just say &quot;Do this,&quot; it says &quot;Do this because of and.&quot;
