1.2. Product Design Doc Blueprint
1.2.1. Strategic Design Rationale
The design philosophy of DAX Copilot is anchored in "Cognitive Unburdening".1 The central user friction it addresses is the "pajama time" phenomenon—clinicians spending hours after clinic documenting care. To solve this, DAX must effectively "disappear" during the clinical encounter to reinforce the "Invisible Scribe" myth frame.6 The UI is designed to be minimal and low-intervention during the patient visit, expanding into a high-utility, rich editing interface only after the patient has left the room. This dichotomy—Minimalist Capture vs. Maximalist Review—is the core UX driver.
1.2.2. Target User Persona
Primary User: Ambulatory Specialists and Primary Care Physicians (PCPs).
Psychographic Profile: Experienced clinicians suffering from high burnout; they value eye contact and human connection but feel tethered to the computer. They are skeptical of voice technology due to past failures with legacy dictation software.
Technical Environment: High variation; users may move between mobile devices in exam rooms and desktop workstations in private offices.7
1.2.3. Functional Architecture & User Flow
The user journey is divided into three distinct phases: Pre-Encounter (Context), Encounter (Ambient Capture), and Post-Encounter (Review & Commit).
Phase 1: Pre-Encounter (The "Doorway" Moment)
Platform: Mobile App (iOS/Android).1
UI Component: The "Schedule Stream."
Behavior: The app pulls the daily schedule from the Epic/Cerner/Athena EHR.
Context Injection: Before entering the room, the clinician glances at the "Patient Card," which displays a generative AI summary of the last visit and relevant interval events (e.g., "Seen 3 months ago for HTN; BP was uncontrolled. Labs pending.").
Action: Clinician taps "Start Encounter" as they knock on the door.
Phase 2: The Encounter (Ambient Mode)
Visual Design: The screen transitions to a "Dark Mode" or "Dimmed State" to discourage screen gazing.
Visual Feedback: A subtle, organic waveform animation pulses at the bottom of the screen. This provides reassurance that the system is listening without drawing attention.9
Interaction Model:
Passive Listening: The phone sits on the desk or is clipped to a lanyard. It uses beamforming algorithms to separate the doctor's voice from the patient's.
Active Dictation Triggers: The clinician can pivot to "dictation mode" by saying a wake word (e.g., "Hey DAX, note that...") to insert specific physical exam findings that aren't naturally conversational.
Phase 3: Post-Encounter (The "Copilot" Review)
Platform: Desktop Application (integrated into Dragon Medical One sidebar).1
Notification: Within seconds of the encounter ending, a "Draft Ready" notification appears on the desktop.
UI Layout (The Split-Screen Review):
Left Pane (The Note): The fully formatted SOAP note.
Right Pane (The Evidence): The raw transcript, indexed by time.
Key Feature: Audio Evidence Linking: When the clinician clicks on a sentence in the generated HPI (e.g., "Patient reports chest pain began while gardening"), the system highlights the corresponding lines in the transcript and plays back that specific audio snippet. This builds trust and allows for rapid verification.2
Key Feature: Conversational Editing: A chat interface at the bottom allows the doctor to refine the note using natural language: "DAX, make the history more concise and add a follow-up for lipid screening."
